When I first read the task, I understood that unlike previous tasks, 
I wasn't writing implementation code — I was writing tests for code that already existed. 
This was a different mindset. Instead of thinking "how do I build this", I had to think 
"how do I verify this works correctly and breaks in the right way".

Working through this task shifted how I think about code — writing tests forced me to read 
each function not as something to build but as something to break, and that perspective 
change revealed details I would have missed otherwise: that async errors need `await` to be 
caught properly, that random behavior makes tests flaky without mocking, that throttle caches 
results across tests unless explicitly cancelled, and that the real challenge of testing isn't
knowing the Jest API but understanding the code deeply enough to know what assumptions to verify and what edge cases to cover.